The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
if (newmsg->recv_msg)
    deliver_err_response(intf, newmsg->recv_msg, cc);
else
    ipmi_free_smi_msg(newmsg);
goto restart;
```
Reason:
When `sender()` fails, the code only frees `newmsg` if `recv_msg` is NULL; otherwise it calls `deliver_err_response()` (which queues the message) and then jumps to `restart` **without** clearing `intf->curr_msg` or freeing `newmsg`. This means the same message remains as `intf->curr_msg` and `newmsg` is still valid, so on the next loop iteration the same message is sent again. If it fails again, `deliver_err_response()` is called a second time with the same `recv_msg`, causing a double list_add corruption and subsequent use-after-free.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
if (newmsg->recv_msg)
    deliver_err_response(intf, newmsg->recv_msg, cc);
if (!run_to_completion)
    spin_lock_irqsave(&intf->xmit_msgs_lock, flags);
intf->curr_msg = NULL;
if (!run_to_completion)
    spin_unlock_irqrestore(&intf->xmit_msgs_lock, flags);
ipmi_free_smi_msg(newmsg);
newmsg = NULL;
goto restart;
```
Reason:
After a sender failure, the fix explicitly sets `intf->curr_msg = NULL` (under the appropriate lock) so that the next loop iteration will attempt to fetch a new message from the queue. It also always calls `ipmi_free_smi_msg(newmsg)` and sets `newmsg = NULL`, preventing the same message from being reused/freed incorrectly. This eliminates the double-queue and use-after-free by ensuring the failed message is properly cleaned up before restarting.

